right now for morning rounds our look at  the medical news of the week  first up closing the healthcare gap  affecting children across America this  week the Centers for Disease Control  released a new report highlighting some  of the health issues that our nation's  most vulnerable face and possible ways  to address the disparities in care here  now with more on the report a CBS News  contributor dr. taryn Derulo's Tara what  did this report seek to address exactly  well it's trying to address how do we  get our kids to reach their fullest  potential and that a lot of these health  disparities actually have the foundation  or are rooted in early childhood so  those first eight years are really  critical for setting the stage for what  kind of success a child has what kind of  health a child has how their learning  will develop and so stressors in early  life can affect children neurologically  metabolically immunologically  we're talking about things like poverty  things like instability in their home  life lack of quality education and that  can really create a trajectory for a  child so that they become at-risk or  vulnerable and so we know for example  that socioeconomic status is associated  with language development as early as 18  months that vocabulary in a  three-year-old will predict how well  they read in third grade which will then  go on to predict whether they graduate  from high school which then has effects  on chronic diseases so kids who don't  graduate from high school tend to have  more chronic diseases like diabetes so  all of these things are very important  and something that we just aren't early  with it didn't the report identify ways  to sort of address these vulnerable  children so they talked about trying to  pick this up early so that we can refer  kids early for intervention and they  talked about screening for health  disparities socially as well they talked  about identifying kids who may have  hearing issues developmental issues  motor issues and trying to get them  again initiated into treatment early on  and then they also talked about this  idea that just because you identify that  a child is vulnerable doesn't  necessarily mean that they will get into  a program because a lot of the programs  have strict eligibility criteria so what  we need is more programs like for  example what they reference a program  called helped me grow where there's  really kind of a one information line  that parents can call and really help a  child get  with the right program did this the  study look at what doctors specifically  and good knew because of what this  report is obviously about children but  it affects the parents well it did and  so again it placed a big role on doctors  for early identification and early  referral for treatment in particular  they also talked about behavioral issues  and mental health and so we know that in  this country about only 20% of kids with  behavioral issues are getting helped  mental service have mental health help  and so that needs to be addressed and  then they talked about a two-generation  approach basically means we're not just  targeting the child we have to target  the parents we have to look at the whole  family so screening and treating for  things like maternal depression teaching  parents about self-care for themselves  teaching parenting strategies if you  treat the whole family you're gonna do a  better job at helping the child down the  road they imprint on all the things that  we do our next topic the ongoing battle  against multiple sclerosis it's  debilitating debilitating disease that  impacts an estimated 400,000 Americans  and there is currently no cure the new  research at New York's Mount Sinai  Hospital is examining how diet can  potentially play a role in the fight  against MS our Tatara takes a closer  look we'll just go around and and hear  an update from everyone once a month  this pioneering group of MS patients  meets to get tips from a nutritionist  when grains are refined they're stripped  of this outer layer and share their  temptations it was craving hamburgers  I'm terrible the other day they had  lobster crabs and there was  just no way I was gonna test that off  neurologist Ilana Katz and is leading  one of the first clinical trials to  study the link between what we eat gut  bacteria and MS symptoms why did you  decide to investigate diet and it's  linked to ms the gut is actually kind of  a natural place to look and that's  because the immune system about 70% of  it lives inside the gut and has  far-reaching implications throughout the  rest of the body in ms inflammation  occurs when immune cells attack the  brain and spinal cord  this study is testing whether a dietary  intervention can reprogram the immune  system to slow down the assault on  itself so this right here is a very  typical looking spot for MS Karine  Providence and the other patients  enrolled in the trial are following a  strict Mediterranean style diet no  processed food dairy or meat but lots of  fruits vegetables and whole grains why  did you choose that type of diet we  think that those are anti-inflammatory  and we also think that they're neuro  protective symptoms like fatigue  difficulty walking vision problems and  cognitive changes can be severe and  disabling but it can take a very heavy  toll on patients I was really fearful  because you know I have a family have my  children I have my husband and I felt  like as if my life would just be over  with the help of medication Kareem is  still able to work nights as an oncology  nurse four years after her diagnosis  have a pleasant day for her sticking to  the diet is a small sacrifice for better  health no chocolate chip cookies no  dairy no vanilla ice cream that I love  so much but I mean if I can participate  in a diet that can change my life  give me longevity give me a piece of how  I was before I don't mind  well fascinating Tara how unusual is it  to approach treating MS through through  diet it is it is new but it's something  that's happening across a lot of  different fields in medicine you know  one of the things that came up when we  talk to these patients is giving them a  sense of control that's what this is  doing and for diseases like MS  they patients mentioned they may look  well on the outside to people people may  think they're not sick but they suffer  from things like inability to walk bowel  or bladder incontinence trouble with  their vision it really is or can be a  very crippling and disabling disease so  giving them something very small like  changing their diet allows them to feel  empowered just so important to set in  the piece of small sacrifice but it  makes a big difference a huge difference  all right finally using technology to  improve the lives of children with  cerebral palsy these children commonly  experience something called Crouch gait  it's due in part to abnormalities of the  hips knees or ankles and can impact  range of motion making basic things like  walking difficult a team of researchers  at Columbia University's Department of  engineering tested the use of a cable  driven robotic training device the  device applies force on the pelvis  research published in the journal  Science Robotics showed not only  improvements to posture but also  strengthening of the children's lower  limbs it's fascinating that robotics can  be used like this again it is and when  you think of the numbers about four out  of a thousand school-aged kids are  diagnosed with cerebral palsy and this  abnormal gait or abnormal walking can  lead to joint degeneration down the road  where we stand now we don't have a lot  of options physical therapy wise or  strengthening exercise to offer so  something like this could make a really  big impact  fascinating dr. tara Narula thanks
 

